### PASS B: AXIS0-ONLY STEERED ###

Imagine you're sitting in a fast-moving car with a small cup in one of your hands. As the car moves forward at high speed, air rushes past your hand and into the cup. If you simply hold your hand out straight, the air pushes against it but doesn't slow down much because there's not enough resistance to significantly decelerate your movement.

Now, let’s say you decide to cup your hand slightly as you extend it from the window. By doing this, you’re creating more surface area for the air to push against. The air still rushes by, but now instead of just hitting the flat side of your hand, it has to flow around that curved shape. This creates more friction or drag force acting on your hand, which resists its motion through the air.

In the same way, when someone jumps from an airplane without a parachute, they experience very little air resistance due to their relatively small exposed surface area compared to their mass. They fall quickly under gravity alone.

However, if deploying a parachute, imagine it like as being similar to cupping both hands out of the car window. A parachute is essentially many layers of fabric spread out over a large area. When deployed, it dramatically increases the person's effective "surface area" facing the wind (or in freefall, the air). 

This increased exposure means the air must travel further to pass around the parachute, generating significant upward lift forces and massive amounts of drag. These forces work together to counteract the downward pull of gravity, slowing the descent considerably—much like how cupping your hands would make it harder for something to rush past them while driving.

So, just as cupping your hands can resist airflow and slow down objects passing through them, a parachute does the same thing on a larger scale, effectively “catching” the air and slowing the human body during a fall.

